+# Native contact snapshots and Genesis precision
+
+[English](contact-readback.md) | [中文](../zh/contact-readback.md)
+
+## Public boundary
+
+`SimBackend.get_contact_capabilities()` declares complete rigid-contact readback separately from aggregate contact sensors. `get_contact_snapshot()` returns a detached `ContactSnapshot`. Unsupported adapters raise `UnsupportedCapabilityError`; an empty successful result is reserved for an observed absence of contacts. Missing normal, signed distance, contact torque or body net-force readback is `None`, never a zero substitute. `get_capabilities()` includes these instance declarations with source provenance; declaration is not runtime verification.
+
+Arrays identify the environment and public geometry IDs (`get_geom_names()`), positions, forces on both geometries, and optional normals pointing A to B, signed distance (negative penetration) and torque on B about the contact point. SI units and world frames apply. State time, collision geometry time and force interval are separate arrays; clients must not attach post-integration poses to an earlier contact evaluation silently. Reset rows are unavailable until a subsequent solve, and padded native slots are excluded using native counts. Records copy their input arrays and reject invalid identity, shape, nonfinite values and impossible epochs.
+
+The Genesis implementation translates the 1.4.3 padded native collider buffer inside UniSim, retains the last solve without recomputing physics, and binds identities through the common entity layout. It is restricted to mapped rigid scenes and full step barriers; older runtime versions retain existing pair sensors and do not declare the new capability. This source implementation still requires exact-version runtime qualification before downstream production migration. No other adapter claims support yet.
+
+Primitive collision identity uses the owning body, native shape/dimensions and local pose. Cached meshes can omit names or retain another instance's name, so names alone do not identify them. For 1.4.3 aligned free roots, the adapter transforms the built geometry back into the authored link frame using each variant's native anchor, including fixed child links. Ambiguous matches remain unsupported. This cold-path identity check does not change native alignment or solver tolerances.
+
+## Precision and session ownership
+
+`GenesisBackend(..., device="auto", precision="32", seed=None)` retains the existing defaults. Select `device="cpu", precision="64", seed=0` explicitly for a CPU FP64 process. Metadata, NumPy caches, controls, reset values, contact reductions and physics-state exports preserve the selected precision. The existing device-resident tensor lane remains FP32; FP64 does not advertise that separate capability.
+
+Portable Genesis XML export restores already explicit inertials (mass, COM, orientation and diagonal inertia) from the compiled FP64 source model, because the compiled XML writer otherwise rounds these values to six significant digits. Implicit inertials remain implicit. This safeguard does not claim lossless serialization of every MJCF field; actual model parameters still require native readback and qualification.
+
+The host clock uses an integer step count and a per-environment restored origin, avoiding accumulated floating-point addition that can move a sample across a scoring-window boundary. Selected resets and playback rebase only the affected clocks. A device-to-host barrier preserves the device lane's reported time as the new origin; this does not improve the separate tensor lane's clock precision or prove physical trajectory equivalence.
+
+Genesis has one process-wide session. All constructed scenes must share the selected device, precision, seed and `use_deterministic_algorithms` choice (default `False`). An explicit `True` forwards Genesis’ native deterministic policy, including its process-wide TorchScript fusion settings; use a dedicated process for that policy. Conflicting requests fail before changing the active CUDA device or creating another session. CPU cannot specify `device_id`; explicit GPU requires CUDA. After destruction, use a new process. A seed applies during initialization; Python, NumPy and Torch caller RNG states and Torch default device/dtype are restored. A seeded constructor alone does not prove deterministic replay.
+
+`get_import_report()` caches native precision/device, materialized rigid options, gravity and available entity mass/inertia/drive readbacks with engine provenance. Unmeasured common fields stay unknown. These are initial values; they do not describe subsequent domain randomization or prove command/output equivalence. The default optional dependency pin and legacy runtime qualification are unchanged by this interface work.
+
+## Native observations and explicit configuration
+
+Use `genesis_friction_cone="elliptic"` with `create_backend("genesis", ...)`; `friction_cone` is the direct `GenesisBackend` constructor spelling. The factory rejects unprefixed integrator, constraint solver, friction cone, solver iteration and device-ID keywords instead of silently replacing them. `native_friction_cone` in the import report records the requested and actual native enum names; callers can refuse a mismatch before stepping.
+
+`body_ids` and `body_net_force` are paired optional fields: public body columns and `(num_envs, bodies, 3)` world forces. Genesis reads the native aggregate independently from the per-contact ledger, permitting a real force-balance audit. Unavailable reset rows contain zero placeholders with `available=False`; they are not measurements. Do not reconstruct a missing aggregate from per-contact sums and label it native readback.
+
+`actuated_armature=None` and `noslip_iterations=None` preserve native import defaults. Explicit nonnegative values apply to source-actuated DOFs and the rigid solver respectively, before initial property binding. They are backend-specific settings, not a claim of cross-engine equivalence. Native force ranges encode unbounded ends as `"-inf"` / `"+inf"`; NaN remains an error. A native infinite plane has no finite collision-size readback; `get_geom_size()` refuses that query instead of returning visualization bounds.
+
+The 1.4.3 profile retains native inertia alignment. It rejects `body_mass`, `base_mass_delta`, `body_ipos` and `base_com_offset` randomization before mutation pending native setter/frame qualification; the pinned 1.3.3 profile remains unchanged. This explicit capability limit does not prevent fixed-model experiments. Initial import reports do not authorize historical evidence relabeling or establish physical equivalence.
+
+## Validation boundary
+
+Contract tests exercise FP64 distinctions smaller than one FP32 unit, contact orientation, selected reset masking, padded/stale slots, unmapped geometry refusal, detached storage and session conflicts. Tests using fake runtime data establish translation behavior only. Physical equivalence requires a frozen model/configuration, native and UniSim trajectories, full contact records, independent scoring and reset replay in the same official runtime. Preserve failed attempts and version-specific evidence.
